//BruteForce Approach 

Key-Idea:
Generate all possible combinations of n opening and n closing brackets.

For n = 3:
Total characters = 6
Generate all 2⁶ = 64 possibilities.
Check each string using a balance counter.
Keep only valid parentheses strings.

Complexity	
Brute Force
Time	O(n × 2²ⁿ) = O(n × 4ⁿ)
Auxiliary Space	O(n)
Output Space	O(n × Cₙ)
Total Space	O(n × Cₙ)

Code :->

class Solution {
    public List<String> generateParenthesis(int n) {
        List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();
        generateAll("", n, res);
        return res;
    }

     public boolean isValid(String s) {
        int balance = 0;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '(') balance++;
            else balance--;
            if (balance < 0) return false;
        }
        return balance == 0;
    }

    public void generateAll(String curr, int n, List<String> res) {
        if (curr.length() == 2 * n) {
            if (isValid(curr)) res.add(curr);
            return;
        }
        generateAll(curr + "(", n, res);
        generateAll(curr + ")", n, res);
    }

}




//Optimal Approach

Key-Idea:
Don't generate invalid strings in the first place.

Keep track of:

open = number of ( used
close = number of ) used

Time Complexity: O(2^n) (Catalan number): C(n) = (2n)! / (n!(n+1)!) is the number of valid sequences.
Each sequence takes O(n) to build.
So, total complexity: O(C(n) × n)

Space Complexity: O(n) recursion depth.
O(C(n) × n) to store results.

Code:->

class Solution {
      public void backtrack(String curr, int open, int close, int n, List<String> res) {
        if (curr.length() == 2 * n) {
            res.add(curr);
            return;
        }
        if (open < n) backtrack(curr + "(", open + 1, close, n, res);
        if (close < open) backtrack(curr + ")", open, close + 1, n, res);
    }

    public List<String> generateParenthesis(int n) {
        List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack("", 0, 0, n, res);
        return res;
    }

}
